From 6db302614f422c1fc45b1c5498c0615d6f2b034e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:25:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 378/488] soh: name the 44 address-named symbols defined in
 audio_general.c

SoH carries the Ocarina of Time decomp, in which several thousand symbols are
still named after their ROM address. This commit names every such symbol DEFINED
IN ONE FILE, so the work can be reviewed a file at a time.

The file names the unit; it does not bound it. Each rename is applied wherever
the symbol occurs -- the declaration in the header, the definition, and every
call site -- so this commit reaches into whatever other files use these symbols
and leaves no dangling reference. It is a pure rename: no behaviour changes, and
the only edits that are not renames are the provenance comments described below.

Two name sources are used, and every name says which it came from:

  * zeldaret/oot -- SoH's decomp IS zeldaret/oot at the same ROM addresses, so
    where oot has already named a symbol, that name is adopted verbatim. The
    definition gets a "Name from zeldaret/oot" comment and every declaration a
    short "// was <addr> [oot]" tag.

  * DEDUCED -- oot leaves a great many symbols address-named too, so those
    names are inferred from the body and its callers, and are marked as OURS
    rather than passed off as upstream. The definition gets
    "LLM generated name (HIGH|GUESS), was <addr>: <reason>" and the short tag
    reads "[LLM:HIGH]" or "[LLM:GUESS]". A GUESS is tagged at every call site
    as well, not only the declaration, so a reader who meets the name anywhere
    knows it is inferred. "git grep '[LLM:'" lists every place the decomp
    leans on an inference.

The justification recorded for each individual name follows, one entry per
symbol, in the order the renames were made. Nothing is summarised away: these
are the per-commit reasons from the unsquashed history, with the boilerplate
that was identical in every one of them hoisted into the text above.

All 44 are defined in soh/src/code/audio_general.c; updating their references touches 43 files in total.

  * func_800F3138 -> Audio_Noop1
    deduced, confidence GUESS: empty one-argument function; nothing in the
    tree records what it was for

  * func_800F3140 -> Audio_Noop2
    deduced, confidence GUESS: empty two-argument function; nothing in the
    tree records what it was for

  * func_800F314C -> Audio_SetMainBgmIoPort
    deduced, confidence GUESS: queues an 0x82 command on the main BGM player
    with the argument in the port byte and a value of 1; the port's meaning
    is not established

  * func_800F37B8 -> Audio_ComputeSoundFilter
    deduced, confidence HIGH: returns the low-pass filter index for a
    surround-mode sound from its Z depth and pan, in the style of the
    neighbouring Audio_ComputeSound* helpers

  * func_800F3990 -> Audio_ComputeSoundHeightParam
    deduced, confidence GUESS: maps a sound's Y position onto 0-127 with the
    low bit forced, and the result goes out as a per-channel parameter in
    surround mode; what the receiving channel does with it is not
    established

  * func_800F3F84 -> Audio_SetSfxScalesFromSpeed
    deduced, confidence HIGH: turns a speed into the shared frequency and
    volume scales, saturating above 6

  * func_800F4010 -> Audio_PlayWalkSfx
    deduced, confidence GUESS: scales the sfx by the speed and may add a
    second randomised sound; its only caller passes the player's
    floorSfxOffset from the camera, so the walking reading comes from that
    caller rather than from the body

  * func_800F4138 -> Audio_PlaySfxWithSpeedScales
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays a sound with the frequency and volume
    scales derived from the caller's speed

  * func_800F4190 -> Audio_PlaySfxWithFixedScales
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays a sound with the file's constant
    frequency scale and reverb

  * func_800F436C -> Audio_PlaySfxScaledByRate
    deduced, confidence HIGH: scales the volume by a machine's motion rate,
    compressing it below 0.75 and skipping the sound entirely below 0.5

  * func_800F4414 -> Audio_PlaySfxAtInterval
    deduced, confidence HIGH: fires the sound on a countdown whose period
    shortens as the caller's rate rises

  * func_800F44EC -> Audio_SetSfxIntervalRange
    deduced, confidence HIGH: arms that countdown and stores the two bounds
    its period is computed from

  * func_800F4524 -> Audio_PlaySfxWithReverb
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays a sound with a caller-supplied reverb
    and default scales

  * func_800F4578 -> Audio_PlaySfxWithVolume
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays a sound with a caller-supplied volume
    scale and default frequency and reverb

  * func_800F45D0 -> Audio_PlayFishingReelSfx
    deduced, confidence HIGH: drives NA_SE_IT_FISHING_REEL_SLOW on the
    interval timer with a rate-scaled companion sound

  * func_800F4C58 -> Audio_SetSfxIoForActiveSfxId
    deduced, confidence HIGH: sends channel io value 6 to every SFX channel
    currently playing the given sfx id

  * func_800F4E30 -> Audio_PanBgmToNearestSource
    deduced, confidence HIGH: keeps the nearest positional BGM source and
    pans and attenuates every main BGM channel by its X and distance

  * func_800F5ACC -> Audio_PlayMiniBossBgm
    deduced, confidence HIGH: stacks the current main BGM and starts the
    caller's sequence; its own printf calls this the middle boss BGM

  * func_800F5BF0 -> Audio_PlayNatureAmbienceWithRestore
    deduced, confidence HIGH: stacks the current main BGM before starting a
    nature ambience sequence

  * func_800F64E0 -> Audio_SetPauseState
    deduced, confidence HIGH: records the pause flag and plays the window
    open or close sound; kaleido passes 1 on opening and 0 on closing

  * func_800F6964 -> Audio_FadeOutAllPlayers
    deduced, confidence HIGH: fades the main BGM, the fanfare player and
    every channel over the caller's duration

  * func_800F6B3C -> Audio_StartSfxSequence
    deduced, confidence HIGH: starts sequence 0 on the SFX player

  * func_800F6BB8 -> Audio_GetActiveNoteCount
    deduced, confidence HIGH: forwards AudioThread_GetActiveNoteCount

  * func_800F6BDC -> Audio_StopAllSeqAndWait
    deduced, confidence HIGH: disables every sequence and spins until no
    notes are left playing

  * func_800F7170 -> Audio_RestartAudio
    deduced, confidence GUESS: restarts the SFX sequence and pushes the mute
    and reset commands through; whether this belongs to a spec change or
    another reset path is not established

  * func_800F71BC -> Audio_ResetForSpecChange
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the reset audio_seq.c runs when the audio spec
    changes: resets the data, ocarina staffs, channel state, sequences and
    sounds

  * func_800F7208 -> Audio_ResetSequencesAndSfx
    deduced, confidence HIGH: resets the active sequences and channel state
    and restarts the SFX player

  * D_801305B0 -> sFixedFreqScale
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the constant frequency scale
    Audio_PlaySfxWithFixedScales passes

  * D_801305B4 -> sFixedReverb
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the constant reverb that goes with it

  * D_801305B8 -> sSfxIntervalCounter
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the countdown Audio_PlaySfxAtInterval fires on

  * D_801305BC -> sSfxIntervalLimit
    deduced, confidence HIGH: subtracted from the base in that period
    formula, so the fastest period is base minus this

  * D_801305C0 -> sSfxIntervalBase
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the period at full rate, and the constant the
    rate-scaled part is added to

  * D_80130608 -> sAudioPaused
    deduced, confidence HIGH: set while the pause menu is open, and read to
    hold off the river-sound BGM restore

  * D_80130650 -> sNearestBgmSourceDist
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the distance of the source
    Audio_PanBgmToNearestSource is currently following

  * D_8016B7A8 -> sSpeedVolScale
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the volume scale Audio_SetSfxScalesFromSpeed
